            </div>
          </li>
        </ul>

        <br class="my-3">
        <h3 class="text-lightblue">Your Toolkit: What Can You Actually Do?</h3>
        <p>Bali’s conservation scene is diverse. You can get your hands dirty building new reefs, become a data-gathering scientist, or perform critical 'housekeeping' that saves lives. Here’s what your 'work' underwater will look like.</p>
        
        <br class="my-3">
        <h3 class="text-lightblue"><i class="fa-solid fa-coral mr-2" aria-hidden="true"></i>Coral Reef Restoration: The Gardeners of the Sea</h3>
        <p>This is tangible, hands-on work. In areas like Amed, Tulamben, and especially Pemuteran—home to the world's largest Biorock reef restoration project—you can become a coral gardener. Your dives will involve cleaning algae off nursery structures with a toothbrush, using cable ties to attach new, resilient coral fragments ('frags') to metal frames, and meticulously monitoring the growth of existing colonies. It's patient, detailed work, but witnessing a barren patch of rubble transform into a bustling juvenile reef over time is an unparalleled reward.</p>

        <br class="my-3">
        <h3 class="text-lightblue"><i class="fa-solid fa-camera-retro mr-2" aria-hidden="true"></i>Citizen Science: Your Dive Becomes Data</h3>
        <p>If you're detail-oriented and love a mission, this is for you. In world-renowned sites like Nusa Penida, dive operators partner with leading research bodies (like the Marine Megafauna Foundation). Your primary task is to become a research assistant. You'll learn to take non-intrusive identification photos of manta rays, capturing the unique spot patterns on their bellies. This data is fed into global databases to track migration, population health, and breeding patterns. It's the critical evidence needed to lobby for expanded Marine Protected Areas (MPAs) and stronger conservation policies.</p>

        <br class="my-3">
        <h3 class="text-lightblue"><i class="fa-solid fa-trash-alt mr-2" aria-hidden="true"></i>Dive Against Debris: The Frontline Cleanup</h3>
        <p>This is the most direct and instantly gratifying form of conservation. You’ll participate in organised underwater cleanups, targeting specific sites. The mission is to remove marine debris, especially plastic waste and deadly 'ghost nets' (discarded fishing gear) that lethally entangle turtles, sharks, and rays. Beyond just cleaning, you'll also help sort and report the data to global organisations like PADI AWARE. This information helps pinpoint pollution sources and hold polluters accountable. It’s a dive that provides an immediate, life-saving benefit to the local ecosystem.</p>
        
        <br class="my-3">
        <h3 class="text-accent" id="faq">Frequently Asked Questions</h3>
